v1.0.54 build 59: AnimationController-driven expand, pre-mounted picker

Real device (Dimensity 7400, Mali-G615) still felt stutter on expand
even after v1.0.54 build 58. The root cause is two compounding issues:

1. AnimatedSize re-runs the parent Column's layout every frame, forcing
   the surrounding CupertinoListSection.insetGrouped + ClipRSuperellipse
   to repaint on each tick. The 220ms animation = ~13 repaint passes
   that all hit the GPU on every expand.

2. First-open cost: the picker widget (CupertinoDatePicker with 3
   ListWheelScrollView columns, or CupertinoPicker) is built from
   scratch when the user taps. That single first build = shader compile
   + 100+ widget instantiations = a 200-1500ms jank frame on first tap.

Fixes:

- Replaced AnimatedSize with manual AnimationController + CurvedAnimation
  + AnimatedBuilder + ClipRect + Align(heightFactor: 0→1). The
  heightFactor interpolates the visible region without forcing parent
  layout. Animation is driven by a single ticker, no per-frame parent
  invalidation.

- Picker body is now ALWAYS mounted (Offstage + TickerMode when not
  expanded). First-build cost is paid once when the modal opens, not
  on every tap. The Offstage tree stays alive in the element tree
  (cheap on memory, free on paint) and TickerMode disables internal
  wheel animations until expanded.

- Dropped AnimatedRotation on chevron. The 250ms rotation was
  overlapping with the size animation and creating a second implicit
  ticker that pushed more frames. Static chevron icon now.

- _PickerBody (costume/size/purpose) was already converted to
  StatefulWidget in v1.0.54 build 58; the controller for
  FixedExtentScrollController now persists across rebuilds so the wheel
  doesn't reset on parent re-layout.

Tradeoffs:
- ~4 picker bodies always in the tree = small memory cost (~50 widgets),
  but layout is lazy (Offstage skips paint and measures with
  heightFactor=0).
- Chevron no longer rotates. The text "Kostum" + value still makes the
  expanded state obvious; the chevron was decorative.
